course: dictionary
lesson: 2351
title: Same word, different meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The clues in the sentence tell you which meaning to use. Crystallise is one of these words. «Salt crystallises as the water dries.» Here it is a science word: the salt forms tiny crystals, hard shapes with flat sides, as the water goes away.
explain: Read the words around it. Dry, water and salt are the clues. Now try your meaning in the sentence: "Salt forms crystals as the water dries" still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: Crystallise can also describe an idea. «Her plan crystallised in her mind.» A plan cannot turn into crystals. Here it means her plan became clear and definite, like a blurry picture coming into focus.
explain: In cooking, «crystallised ginger» is ginger coated with sugar so it is sweet and hard. The same word has three different jobs. Always check the clues around it, then test your meaning.
fact: Crystallise means to form crystals, or to become clear and definite.
fact: When a substance crystallises, it usually forms crystals as a liquid cools or water evaporates.
fact: When a plan crystallises, it becomes clear and definite in your mind.
fact: «Crystallised fruit» means fruit coated with sugar so it becomes sweet and hard. It is used to decorate cakes.
fact: Honey that turns hard and grainy has crystallised, and it is not bad to eat. Warm water melts the crystals.
exercise: «Salt crystallises as the water dries.» What does crystallises mean here? | It becomes clear and definite | *It forms crystals | It melts into a puddle | It gets very hot
exercise: «Her plan crystallised in her mind.» What does crystallised mean here? | *It became clear and definite | It turned to ice | It was lost and forgotten | It formed salt crystals
exercise: In «crystallised ginger», what has happened to the ginger? | It has been left in the sun to dry out | It has been cut into tiny cubes only | It has been frozen solid | *It is coated with sugar so it is sweet and hard
test: «Honey that turns hard and grainy has crystallised.» Is this honey bad to eat? | *No, it has crystallised but it is not bad | It is only bad in summer | Yes, germs have grown in it | Yes, throw it away at once
test: In «crystallised fruit», what does crystallised mean? | *Coated with sugar so it is sweet and hard | Turned into salt | Melted in warm water | Made clear and definite
exam: Which meaning fits «The idea crystallised into a clear plan»? | It melted in warm water | Turned into salt | *It became clear and definite | It was coated with sugar
